				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gopher-chucks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7069900); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7069900&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609773054" alt="Gopher-Chucks" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7069900)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gopher-chucks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7069900); return false;" >Gopher-Chucks</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606606525 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Nov 2020 23:35</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4830696">
									

<p><em>I moved and trimmed your thread summary, since it was taking up extra space in the main forum screen. Try to keep thread descriptions (the text in the little box under the thread title) and titles short so you don't crowd out other authors in the forum listing. ~Zyn</em></p>
<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Horror (body horror)</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Class, containment procedures, description, addenda (including a voiceover mp3 file with sound effects)</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Pregnant woman is found frozen in the arctic, torn in half, fetus missing. Top half of body is dead, lower half moves erratically on its own, possibly with a purpose or cognition.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Corpse was found frozen in Yukon when villagers noticed a strange, unidentified creature moving erratically. Corpse's lower half is controlled by a series of live neurons that consume the bone and ligament tissue and shock the muscles into moving convulsively, allowing it to crawl. It will violently hyperextend its joints (bending knees backwards and such) and break its own bones to move.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The live neuron DNA matches the dead woman, whose brain is somewhat shrunken, or possibly her fetus. Lead scientist believes it's capable of navigation and is searching for something. During observation session, which is recorded as audio file, corpse's bottom half attempts to navigate the facility's halls and leave through the exit, which is locked, then scurries back to its top half in its freezer unit. As scientist closes the freezer door, listeners may hear the corpse vocalize its desire for its father, which is not written in transcript. Scientist does not hear it, dismissing it as his own involuntary vocalization. He regrets that the skip's lifespan is so short as a result of consuming its own flesh and progressively deflating in size, that they'll never know what it wants, so it must remain frozen to stay alive until the foundation finds a way to save it, find what it's looking for, or determine how it came to be.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Using body horror as a focus, of which I haven't seen much here, and a full story with open-ended conclusion that is coherent with beginning, middle, and end if the reader reads between the lines. Specifically, I'd like to include a voiceover file from the scientist's observation session with written transcript, recording the skittering and cracking noises the skip makes, with a subtle hint/easter egg for readers to listen for, revealing what it's capable of, and possibly answering the question of what it actually wants (this won't be included in the transcript, so that the reader is left to think and interpret it on their own).</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Please advise how to include the mp3 file with submission, as it's essential to the storytelling, thanks.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<em>As a courtesy to our readers on mobile devices, please collapse long posts. ~Zyn</em>
