			<div class="content" id="post-content-4831369">
									

<p>I guess I'll explain what I can about the narrative; I wasn't going to, since this is only a brief initial pitch, but hopefully you'll find this to be interesting at least. Here's the plot summary:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm hoping that the reader will interpret that by the absence of fetal tissue in the enlarged uterus and the shrunken portions of the woman's brain that regulate cognitive functions, sensory input, movement, and memory, that the fetus mutated in a way that caused it to absorb the mother's brain functions and dissolve, sinking into the legs with only the nervous system surviving, which means that the nervous system is essentially a hive mind of the mother and fetus. The mother had been wandering as a result of her brain's degradation and died in the snow, being frozen for an indiscriminate amount of time so that it would be difficult to pinpoint when she died. When the corpse thawed, the legs and lower torso forcibly pulled itself away from the upper body, confused due to its overall lack of &quot;brain power,&quot; but with enough sense of direction (likely due to the mutation, or perhaps from the mother's prior knowledge and memory) to know approximately where the nearest village is, but never finds its way there and is instead picked up by the foundation when villagers report seeing it in the distance. It returns to the top half of the corpse before being acquired. The information that helps the reader determine this is revealed in different segments in the description section.</p>
<p>The scientist's observation session, which he commentates for the audio file, involves the scientist explaining that in the last two sessions, it had tried to exit the lab, so for this one, he has evacuated and disinfected the halls to observe what the skip does when it's let out. It blindly but frantically crawls along walls, feeling each doorway before coming to the facility's north exit and being adamant about trying to move through the exit, likely because it can sense the air coming from the outside. After failing to open the door by kicking it, it quickly scurries back to the freezer unit it's kept in and huddles back against its top half, connecting with it like a puzzle piece. As the scientist ends the session and closes the freezer, he hesitates, thinking he saw the top half's arm move, but dismisses it as a trick of light from the leg twitching. As he closes it, a raspy voice whispering, &quot;Need Dad,&quot; can be briefly heard as the door is closing (this is not included in the transcript, only the audio file). Since the entity is likely a hive mind, the reader is left to interpret that it is looking for a specific family member, either the baby's father or the mother's father, who probably lived in the village where it was found. The reader will hopefully interpret that the skip was able to transfer some of its neurons to the top half to briefly control the mouth and vocal cords, and when the skip was initially acquired in two pieces, it was probably attempting to do the same thing. So it longs for closure.</p>
<p>The scientist doesn't hear the voice coming from the top half and dismisses the session as only partly informative about what it can do. He keeps the skip frozen to preserve its lifespan until it can be determined if this was a single mutation or something contagious that can happen again. A second addendum reveals that some researchers who listened to the scientist's recording noticed the indiscriminate sound as he closed the door, but the scientist has failed in his attempts to recreate the circumstances that allowed the whisper to be heard. He dismisses it as a noise that he perhaps made involuntarily as he closed the freezer door, thus dooming the skip to be frozen indefinitely.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I hope this is enough to constitute a beginning, middle, and end for a narrative. If you don't agree or don't find it interesting, let me know.</p>

