<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What are the origins of this anomaly?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The implications I'm making are that the fetus developed a mutation that absorbed the mother's brain matter into itself, then caused the rest of its body to dissolve and sink into the legs. The nervous system in the legs pulls itself away from the top half.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What closure would you think would make sense?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The skip manages to use all its effort in the observation session to make it clear it wants to return to a family member (causing the top half to whisper), but can't manage to do it a second time, and its attempts are thus dismissed as too speculative for the researchers to take action on. So much time is spent on trying to recreate the circumstances to repeat the message it left in the recorded session that it ends up consuming too much of its own body and deflates, becoming sluggish and close to death. The lead scientist decides to freeze it indefinitely until a solid answer on whether the mutation can happen again is found, or if a way to regenerate the consumed tissue is discovered. Essentially it's a downer ending as the skip can't get what it wants, despite its hardships. Because its still technically alive at the end, it's left open-ended, though. Do you think it should not be open-ended?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And then how could you imply those things without maybe explicitly stating them?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The origin is implied in the description section. I mention the shrunken portions of the brain that control cognition, motor functions, memory, etc, without explicitly stating their function- it just says frontal lobe, parietal lobe, and hippocampus without explaining to the reader why that's significant; I don't intend to talk down to them. I mention that the bisecting tear is along the uterus and the pattern of the tear suggests that the woman was pulled apart rather than cut in half, but that there are no markings or scars to suggest that an outside force caused it, leading the reader to consider that something inside her caused the bisection (the fetus, which is listed as cryptically missing or nonexistent). I mention the DNA in the living neurons are a near match to the corpse, implying that it is not a foreign force taking over the legs, but either living remains of the fetus and/or the woman herself.</p>
<p>The conclusion is implied in the addenda. The first addendum is the audio file where the top half's whisper is heard, and the transcript where the whisper is excluded. This is called to the listener's attention beforehand when the scientist stops, claiming he thinks he saw an arm move, implying that there is life in the top half.</p>
<p>The second addendum mentions that other researchers heard the whisper, describing it as an indiscriminate noise, and recommended further research in case it meant something (without mentioning what the whisper said, or that it was even a whisper). The lead scientist recreates the situation several times in hopes that it will happen again and tests for living tissue in the throat and mouth but repeatedly finds nothing and eventually dismisses the whole argument that it made any significant sound. This hints to the reader that it may have spoken something, so they will listen to the audio file closely and try to decipher it on their own.</p>
<p>The third addendum simply mentions that it has consumed itself too much during its time outside the freezer and will be preserved cryogenically, rather than through conventional freezing.</p>
<p>Basically, I'm hoping to get the readers to piece the puzzle together on their own through both reading and listening, which I think would make a unique experience, but that's up to you if you agree.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>so just a gory description of this anomaly isn’t really story rich, or all that unique</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is part of the reason I intend to add the audio file, not just for the story/narrative, but for the sound effects the skip makes, (cracking, sloshing, banging, skittering, etc.) and the scientist's reactions, which I think will add to the discomfort factor (I know audio is what made SCP-682 so infamous, not that my intention is to mimic that article). I also think that the observation session is what will give the skip some personality and characterization. Again, it's up to you if you think this is worth trying.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

